# Dockerfile.dockerignore -- RAMEAU portal build context
#
# Context root: services/portal/ in this repository (the app root, not the repo root).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FILENAME IS LOAD-BEARING, AND IT WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-11-02
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker reads an ignore-file from the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT, or as
# `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile -- and nowhere else. This file lives
# in deploy/images/portal/ while the context is services/portal/, so for as long as it was
# named plain `.dockerignore` it was read by NOTHING and every pattern below was inert:
# node_modules/, dist/ and the stale `index-*.js` build output were all uploaded with the
# context.
#
# This image escaped the worst of that only by luck. Its Dockerfile COPYs an explicit list
# rather than a wildcard, so a planted `index-stale.js` reached neither stage because no COPY
# names it -- whereas the router's `COPY *.py ./` had nothing bounding it. Luck is not the
# design this file describes, and `public/` and `src/` are copied wholesale, which only this
# list bounds.
#
# So do not rename this file back, and do not move it without moving the Dockerfile with it.
# `git mv`-ing either one alone disarms this entire file, silently.
#
# Deny-by-default, then re-admit. Same two syntax rules as the router's list: a '#' is a
# comment only at the start of a line, so never put one after a pattern; and a negation
# cannot re-admit a file whose parent directory is still excluded.

# ---- deny everything, then re-admit ----
**

# ---- the reproducible install ----
!package.json
!package-lock.json

# ---- the Vite entry and its config ----
!index.html
!vite.config.js

# ---- sources and static assets ----
!src/
!src/**
!public/
!public/**

# ---- the nginx config, copied into the serve stage as a template ----
!nginx.conf

# ---- STALE BUILD OUTPUT: never admit ----
# A committed index-*.js / index-*.css pair existed at the app root of the source tree this
# came from, while .gitignore ignored `dist`. Vite writes to dist/, so those files were a
# PREVIOUS build output, not an input. Reaching the build stage they would be harmless;
# reaching the serve stage they would shadow the fresh bundle and the portal would serve a
# stale build under a stale asset hash -- silently, with no error anywhere. Excluded by `**`
# already, and named here so that a future edit re-admitting the app root cannot sweep them
# back in.
assets/
index-*.js
index-*.css
dist/

# ---- never, under any circumstances ----
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
.netrc
.git-credentials
.git/
node_modules/
*.log
*.bak
*.bak-*
*_fixed.jsx
*_v2.jsx
